AWS IAM privilege escalation via `AssumeRole` occurs when a role’s trust policy allows `sts:AssumeRole` for any principal (`Principal:"*"`) with ambiguous conditions. Technical vector:
1. Enumeration: `aws iam list-attached-user-policies --user-name Alice`
2. Discovery: `aws iam list-role-policies --role-name TargetRole`
3. Exploitation: `aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/TargetRole --role-session-name hacker`
4. Abuse: If `TargetRole` has `Action: "*"` in `Resource` within the role’s policy, attacker gains full access.
Terraform misconfiguration:
```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "vulnerable_role" {
  name = "RapidEscalateRole"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect    = "Allow"
      Principal = { AWS = "*" }
      Action    = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
  inline_policy {
    name = "AdminAccess"
    policy = jsonencode({
      Version = "2012-10-17"
      Statement = [{
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Action   = "*"
        Resource = "*"
      }]
    })
  }
}
```
Tools: Pacu (`iam__enum_permissions`), Steampipe (`aws_compliance.iam_star_assume`).
Mitigations: Enforce `aws:userid` condition in trust policy, require MFA, audit via `aws iam simulate-principal-policy`.
Real-world: Capital One breach (2019) was partially enabled by over-permissive IAM roles.

